---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, LABELS } from '../lib/site.mjs';
import { CULTURE_MIN_INSIDE_SHARE, DEVELOPMENT_WINDOW_DAYS } from '../lib/load.mjs';
const base = '';
---
<Base
  title="About and method"
  description="Why Common Ground exists, how it is made, and how sources are chosen, labelled and reviewed."
  base={base}
  current="about"
>
  <main class="about">
    <header class="about-head">
      <p class="label">About and method</p>
      <h1>Understanding comes before agreement</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="statement" aria-labelledby="why">
      <h2 id="why">Why I made this</h2>
      <p>
        AI is being built and governed everywhere, but most of us only follow the conversation in our own country and our own
        language. Making AI safe is a global problem, and I don't think it can be solved by any one country, or by one set of values
        being applied to everyone else. It will need countries to work together, and working together starts with understanding
        each other.
      </p>
      <p>
        Common Ground is an attempt to help with that. For each country it sets out what people there think about AI, what their
        government has done, what the people building AI there care about, and the news that's shaping it now. It doesn't rank
        countries or judge them. The only position it takes is that cooperation is worth working for, and that understanding comes
        before agreement.
      </p>
      <p class="signature">
        <strong>{SITE.editor}</strong>, Editor<br />
        <span>
          James lives and works in the UK and has recently moved into AI safety. James started Common Ground out of a commitment to
          helping the world come together on the biggest challenge of our time.
        </span>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="how">
      <h2 id="how">How it's made</h2>
      <dl class="how">
        <div>
          <dt>Sources for everything</dt>
          <dd>
            Every statement links to its evidence, with a date and labels showing whether the source is from inside or outside the
            country and what kind of source it is. A statement without a source cannot be published: the site's build checks this
            automatically.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Local voices first on culture</dt>
          <dd>
            Sections on a country's culture and industry give priority to sources from that country, including "in their own
            words" quotes from its officials, researchers, companies, unions and media. At least {CULTURE_MIN_INSIDE_SHARE * 100}%
            of a culture section's sources must come from inside the country, or the page says plainly that it falls short. Factual
            claims draw equally on sources from inside and outside.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>AI-assisted, human-approved</dt>
          <dd>
            Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, helps find sources, draft updates and translate pages. Nothing is published until
            the editor has reviewed it, and culture sections are also reviewed by contributors from the country concerned. Because
            Claude is made by Anthropic, anything that mentions Anthropic or its models always gets a second reviewer.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Machine translation, clearly marked</dt>
          <dd>Translated pages will say so, and readers can report problems from any page.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Open and correctable</dt>
          <dd>
            All content and its full edit history are public on <a href={SITE.repo}>GitHub</a>. If something is wrong or unfair,
            please <a href={`${SITE.repo}/issues/new?template=correction.yml`}>tell us</a>. Corrections are logged publicly, and
            anyone described here has a right of reply.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="scope">
      <h2 id="scope">What counts as AI safety here</h2>
      <p id="definition">
        We use a broad definition, following
        <a href="https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-definitions/what-is-ai-safety">Stanford HAI's explainer on AI safety</a>, which
        describes the field as ensuring AI systems behave reliably and don't cause harm. That includes preventing accidents and
        misuse, keeping systems under human control, and building AI that is robust, secure and aligned with human intentions and
        the values of the societies using it.
      </p>
      <p>
        News is included when it is about AI safety or when it affects it. That covers new frontier models and open-weight releases,
        changes to institutions and laws, compute and chip policy, public opinion, and changes in who holds political power.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="labels">
      <h2 id="labels">How sources are labelled</h2>
      <div class="label-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Origin</h3>
          <ul class="plain">
            <li><span class="tag tag-inside">inside</span> {LABELS.origin.inside}</li>
            <li><span class="tag tag-outside">outside</span> {LABELS.origin.outside}</li>
            <li><span class="tag tag-international">international</span> {LABELS.origin.international}, such as the OECD or UN</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Type</h3>
          <p class="tags">{Object.values(LABELS.type).map((t) => <span class="tag">{t}</span>)}</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Language</h3>
          <p>The source's original language, plus <span class="tag">machine-translated</span> when we translated it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Evidence depth</h3>
          <p>
            Each section says whether evidence is rich, moderate or limited, and lists the gaps we know about, so a short page reads
            as a gap in the evidence rather than a lack of activity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="review">
      <h2 id="review">How content is reviewed</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Section</th><th scope="col">Updated</th><th scope="col">Review</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>News</td><td>Weekly</td><td>Light: the editor checks the headline, why it matters, and the source</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Frameworks and regulation</td><td>Monthly</td><td>Medium: each change checked against the primary source</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Public attitudes</td><td>When new polls appear</td><td>Medium: figures, sample and method checked against the release</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Culture and industry</td><td>Quarterly</td><td>Strict: the editor and a local reviewer read it in full</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Sensitive topics</td><td>As needed</td><td>Strict: the editor and a second reviewer; sources from more than one side</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        News is marked as a recent development only if it is less than {DEVELOPMENT_WINDOW_DAYS} days old when checked. Older stories
        that explain the current picture are marked foundational. Automated checks also flag words that tend to judge rather than
        describe, such as "lagging" or "draconian", for the editor to look at.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="maps">
      <h2 id="maps">Maps and borders</h2>
      <p>
        Country shapes come from Natural Earth. Borders are shown for navigation only and imply no position on any disputed
        territory. During the pilot the European Union is shown as one area.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contribute">
      <h2 id="contribute">Contribute</h2>
      <p>
        We are looking for people in each covered country to review culture sections, suggest sources in their own languages, and
        check translations. You can contribute under your name or anonymously, and every contributor declares any conflicts of
        interest. <a href={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md`}>Read how to contribute</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-block: 40px 56px;
    padding-left: var(--gutter);
    padding-right: var(--gutter-r);
    display: grid;
    gap: 40px;
  }
  .about-head h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 6vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 0.98;
    margin-top: 6px;
  }
  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
  }
  h3 {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .statement {
    padding: 24px;
    background: var(--paper-2);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .signature {
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .signature strong {
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .how {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 16px;
  }
  .how dt {
    font-family: var(--font-head);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: var(--step-1);
  }
  .how dd {
    margin: 2px 0 0;
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }
  .label-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
  }
  .plain {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 6px;
  }
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 520px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
    padding: 8px 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  th {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-3);
  }
</style>
